# Shell scripts run under /bin/sh inside the Linux service images — every
# service Dockerfile COPYs its db-init.sh in and the CMD runs it. A CRLF
# checkout (git's core.autocrlf=true is the Windows default) makes the shell
# read `set -e` with a trailing carriage return, which fails as
# "set: illegal option -" and crash-loops all nine containers on boot.
# These must stay LF no matter how the developer's git is configured.
*.sh text eol=lf

# .prettierrc.json sets endOfLine: "lf". Without this, core.autocrlf=true
# (git's Windows default) checks these out as CRLF, and `prettier --check`
# then reports every single line of the file as a style violation — not a
# real formatting problem, just the checkout disagreeing with the config. It
# reads as "the whole repo fails Lint & format" when only this file's
# checkout does.
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf

# Not caught by the prettier/CI reasoning above (`*.json` isn't in that
# --check glob) — this is a DEPLOY-time bug instead: `git archive` run on
# Windows (core.autocrlf=true) applies the same text-conversion a checkout
# would, so a plain `git archive` of a new .json file — e.g. web/public/
# manifest.json, added for PWA installability — came out CRLF even though
# the committed blob is LF, while scp'd straight to the Linux deploy
# directory with no checkout step to convert it back. Harmless for JSON
# parsing itself, but pointless drift from the committed content once it is
# actually deployed, and confusing when hash-verifying a deploy against the
# source finds a diff that reads as identical.
*.json text eol=lf
